5,Music of Melanesia — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/music_of_melanesia,"The music of Melanesia encompasses the diverse traditional and contemporary musical practices of the indigenous peoples across the island chains of Papua New Guinea, the Solomon Islands, Vanuatu, Fiji, and New Caledonia, regions marked by over 1,100 languages and profound cultural variation.",Isebe,Trans-New Guinea
6,Isebe - igo (Trans-New Guinea) - PeopleGroups.org,https://peoplegroups.org/language/igo/,"Isebe (igo) is part of the Trans-New Guinea family and Trans-New Guinea, Madang, Croisilles, Mabuso, Gum, Panim-Isebe class. Isebe speaking People Groups across the world: Fast Facts",Isebe,Trans-New Guinea
7,Trans New Guinea [Newguineaworld],https://newguineaworld.linguistik.uzh.ch/families/trans-new-guinea/start,"Trans New Guinea. Timothy Usher, Santa Fe Institute.Awaiting the conclusion of lexical comparison between its reconstructed subfamilies, the most compelling evidence for Trans-New Guinea is the formal resemblances between the personal pronouns of its members.",Isebe,Trans-New Guinea
8,Isebe language — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/isebe_language,"Isebe is classified as a member of the Trans-New Guinea phylum, within the Madang branch, specifically the Croisilles linkage and Mabuso subgroup, and further placed in the Gum group alongside languages such as Amele, Bau, and Panim.",Isebe,Trans-New Guinea
